  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nTemperatures this afternoon have climbed into the upper 70s and low \n80s but humidity is fairly low thanks to drier air in place. A few \npop up showers may be possible this afternoon across the Twin Tiers. \nGiven the dry air in place, these showers will be light and should \nnot amount to much precipitation. Any showers that can develop \nshould come to an end later this evening. Overnight, winds will \nbecome much calmer and that should lead to fog developing, at least \nin the valleys. There will be some higher cloud cover that may delay \nthe timing of fog development early Friday morning. Temperatures \nwill fall into the 40s and 50s. \n\nHigh pressure will stick around through Friday with another pleasant \nday in store. Under northwesterly flow, temperatures will only climb \ninto the 70s with dew points in the 50s. Dew points were lowered \nwith the help of Consall guidance. It will be a cool and potentially \nfog night with temperatures falling into the 40s and 50s.\n\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA warm front will move through the region late Saturday with the \nnext round of showers. With the later timing, instability will be \nquite limited but an isolated thunderstorm or two cannot be ruled \nout in Northeast PA and the Catskills. A cold front will soon follow \nwith additional showers and storms. Models are showing around 500 to \n1000 J/kg of MLCAPE. If there is a break in showers and some \nclearing skies early in the day, then these values may be possible. \nCombined with 30 to 35 kts of shear, some gusty thunderstorms\nwould then be possible Sunday afternoon. Mid-level lapse rates \nare lacking so severe storms are not expected at this time. \nShowers will linger into Sunday night and possibly into Monday \ndepending on how quickly the front moves through. High pressure \nwill build in behind the departure of this system. Besides some \nuncertainty with timing, there is also uncertainty with the \nnorthward coverage. Models seem to agree that the bulk of \nprecipitation will likely be over southern portions of the \nregion. Localized rainfall amounts of 1 inch or more will be \npossible. Further north, amounts will be less than 1/2 inch.\n\nA slight warming trend is expected through the weekend before colder \nair then fills in behind the departing cold front. Some will \nstruggle to reach 70 on Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nHigh pressure will keep the start of Tuesday dry as temperatures \nwill rebound back into the 70s. The high pressure then slides \neastward, allowing for the next system to move in. This system will \nmove in from the northwest with showers entering north-central NY by \nTuesday night with northeast PA staying dry until later in the day \nWednesday. There is some uncertainty with the arrival of this system \nand how quick precipitation spreads southward. Areas that can stay \nrain-free early Wednesday may also see afternoon thunderstorms, \nthough instability may be limited.",
